o(4,1,'explanation','Given the trusted metainfo in the prompt, a tracker is not a replacement source of piece hashes. A mismatch means these bytes cannot be accepted as the expected piece.')
rep(4,['workedExample'],'If one peer supplied all bad blocks, ban it.','Apply a cautious peer-reputation policy. A mixed-source piece mismatch alone cannot identify the bad contributor; even a sole supplier does not prove deliberate malice.')
o(5,2,'explanation','The tracker role supplies peer lists, rather than the initial data piece. A machine could separately run a seeding service.')
o(5,3,'text','Optimistic unchoking gives a peer an upload opportunity without requiring a prior contribution')
o(5,3,'explanation','In the classic BEP 3 policy an optimistic slot rotates on a 30-second cadence, with bias towards new connections. This creates an opportunity, not a guarantee of rapid completion.')
u(5,['workedExample'],'BEP 3 describes changing choking decisions every ten seconds and rotating an optimistic unchoke every thirty. If that optimistic peer is interested, it counts within the four allowed interested downloaders. New connections are favoured in selection. This lets a newcomer obtain something to trade; seeders and modern client policies may behave differently.')
u(6,['prompt'],'Two WebRTC peers are on different private networks with endpoint-dependent NAT mappings. ICE direct host and server-reflexive checks have failed, but a valid TURN relay path succeeds. Which candidate pair can be selected?')
o(6,0,'explanation','The stated successful relay path supplies connectivity after these direct checks failed. TURN is not guaranteed to work on every network.')
o(6,1,'explanation','Priority cannot make a failed path usable; this prompt explicitly says the direct host checks failed.')
o(6,2,'explanation','The prompt says those checks failed. Endpoint-dependent mappings can make a STUN-observed address unsuitable for another destination, but NAT labels alone do not prove universal failure.')
u(6,['workedExample'],'ICE evaluates actual reachability. In this scenario, direct checks fail and a relay-involving pair succeeds, so the controlling agent can nominate that valid pair. Depending on the selected pair, one or both peers use a TURN allocation. Relay transport must itself be allowed by the network; media traffic consumes relay bandwidth.')
rep(7,['prompt'],'how much does each participant upload?','ignoring protocol overhead and retransmissions, how much does each participant upload?')
