u(9,['workedExample'],'Byte 0x80 gives V=2, P=0, X=0, CC=0. Byte 0xe0 gives M=1 and PT=96. Big-endian sequence 0x0005 is 5; timestamp 0x00015f90 is 90,000; SSRC is 0x12345678.\n\nPT 96 is dynamically mapped; the header alone does not identify the codec or clock rate. A difference of 90,000 ticks means one second at 90 kHz, but this absolute timestamp does not show elapsed stream time without a reference. Marker semantics depend on the negotiated payload format.')
o(10,1,'text','Exponentially sized distance buckets support logarithmic lookup scaling when routing and ID-distribution assumptions hold')
o(10,1,'explanation','The model supports progress through ID prefixes. This is not a promise that every response halves numeric XOR distance or that failed nodes and adversaries cannot disrupt a lookup.')
o(10,2,'text','Parallel queries reduce dependence on one slow or dead contact')
o(10,2,'explanation','Concurrency, such as the paper\'s example α=3, can overlap delays. It does not eliminate all timeouts or guarantee a result.')
u(10,['workedExample'],'XOR symmetry allows incoming messages to teach useful contacts. Maintained distance buckets support prefix progress under the model\'s assumptions. Parallel queries overlap delays, while responsive old contacts are retained rather than automatically replaced by newcomers. If a round finds no closer node, original Kademlia still queries remaining closest candidates before terminating. Actual latency, success and churn behaviour depend on the network; no current typical round count is assumed.')
